# Overview
#
# A self-answered question is a short phrase built on a noun that the
# writer poses as a question and answers in the next sentence (`The
# result? A build that finishes in half the time.`). Rhetoric calls this
# device hypophora. The question holds back the point instead of stating
# it, so the reader waits one more sentence for it. The fix states the
# point (`The build finishes in half the time`). The rule sits at
# Recommended, because a real question that ends on a listed noun also
# matches.
#
# Expression
#
# `setup` lists the nouns that introduce an answer, such as `result`,
# `catch`, and `upshot`. `main` matches `the`, a noun in `setup`, and a
# question mark glued to the noun. It also matches `the best part?`,
# `the worst part?`, `the good news?`, `the bad news?`, and `the short
# answer?`.
#
# False Positives
#
# Nouns that end a real question stay out of `setup` (`What's the
# difference?`, `What is the fix?`, `What was the outcome?`). A real
# question that ends on a listed noun still matches (`What was the
# result?`), because a rule cannot see the start of the sentence.
# Technical prose rarely asks such a question.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit no lines in the measure.py corpus, so no sample measures
# its precision.
expr setup [result, catch, upshot, kicker, verdict, takeaway, twist, payoff, culprit, trick, secret, downside, upside]
expr main [(the @setup?), (the [best, worst] part?), (the [good, bad] news?), (the short answer?)]
let message "Self-answered question. State the point as a sentence instead of asking for it."
let description "Flags a noun phrase posed as a question and answered in the next sentence, such as `The result?` or `The best part?`"
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The result is a build that finishes in half the time."
allows "What's the difference between the two flags?"
